{"text": " You know what else is incredibly infuriating? Well, there's the global pharmaceuticals industry that incentivizes disease because it's more profitable to treat symptoms than address actual causes. There's the global farming industry that cause a lot of the causes because it's more cost-effective to feed us things that hurt us than the things that actually do as good. And they also cause a lot of the starvation because apparently artificial food scarcity is really good for business. There's the global industrial war machine that incentivizes conflict and destabilization because they figured out that getting politicians to keep funneling taxpayer money into their pockets under the guise of defense is basically an infinite money glitch. Then of course there's the other industries that take advantage of that destabilization to get their hands on cheap resources that they use to manufacture their products that they then get to market to us as environmentally friendly. This does not look like a very friendly environment to me. Then of course there's the global political establishment that take money from all of these industries and try and convince us that our only hope to try and fix any of these problems is to keep voting for them and expecting them to do something about it despite that obviously not being in their self-interest. This of course all propped up by a global economic system that incentivizes wasting resources through over-production and over-consumption in order to make money inflating itself by merely existing by wasting the resources that that money derides its value from in the first place. We'll seemingly overseen by some shadowy faceless cabal of banks and investment firms who all own a percentage of everything and all own a percentage of each other in some giant circular economic cluster fuck so it's impossible to trace the money back to anyone and figure out who's actually in charge here. So a lot, quite a lot.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "se